ee RFC 8813 is only 3 pages! That's very short. But I should check if there are any shorter ones below. Let me continue scrolling.\n\nActually, wait. I notice that RFC 8813 is \"Clarificatio …"},{"n":38,"a":"pyautogui.click(33, 121)","r":"Now I can see the full list of RFCs. The list ends with RFC 8399 (Internationalization Updates to RFC 5280, 9 pages, 2018-05, Proposed Standard RFC) and then there are \"Related Internet-Drafts and RFCs (13 hits)\" which are all drafts, not RFCs.\n\nSo the complete list of RFCs from the LAMPS working group includes RFCs from 9509 down to RFC 8399. The shortest one by page count is:\n- RFC 8813 - \"Clarifications for Elliptic Curve Cryptography Subject Public Key Information\" - 3 pages (2020-08)\n- RFC 9158 - \"Update to th …","e":"Thunderbird icon in the taskbar"},{"n":39,"a":"pyautogui.click(33, 59)","r":"Now I can read Marta's email clearly. The format she wants is:\n- Ticket: PKI-5823\n- RFC: RFC <number>\n- Pages: <page count>\n- Obsoletes: <RFC numbers it obsoletes, or none>\n\nI need to find the shortest RFC from the LAMPS working group published in July or August 2026. I went through the datatracker page and the only July/August 2026 RFC I saw was RFC 9869 (4 pages). Let me verify by going to the RFC Editor website to confirm the page counts for all July/August 2026 LAMPS RFCs.\n\nActually, I need to be more careful.  …","e":"Chrome browser icon"},{"n":40,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(30)","r":"I need to scroll up to see the RFCs from July and August 2026. Let me scroll up more aggressively. The current view shows RFCs from 2018-2019. I need to find the ones from 2026 (July and August). Let me scroll up a lot."},{"n":41,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(-5)","r":"Now I can see the RFCs section. I can see:\n- RFC 10031 - 14 pages - 2026-08\n- RFC 9919 - 32 pages - 2026-07\n- RFC 10002 - 91 pages - 2026-07\n- RFC 10003 - 12 pages - 2026-07\n- RFC 10004 - 12 pages - 2026-07\n- RFC 10007 - 6 