g of the list to identify which one was published in July-August 2026 and is the shortest.\n\nFrom what I've seen so far, the LAMPS RFCs published in 2026 include:\n- RFC 10004: Certificate Management over CMS (CMC): Compliance Requirements - July 2026\n- RFC 10003: Certificate Management over CMS (CMC): Transport Protocols - July 2026\n- RFC 10002: Certificate Managemen …"},{"n":30,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(5)","r":"Now I can see the full list of LAMPS RFCs published in 2026. Let me scroll up a bit more to see the beginning of the list.\n\nI can see:\n- RFC 10007: Clarification to Processing Key Usage Values During Certificate Revocation List (CRL) Validation - July 2026, PROPOSED STANDARD\n- RFC 9935: Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure - Algorithm Identifiers for the Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM) - March 2026, PROPOSED STANDARD\n\nAnd from before:\n- RFC 10004: Certificate Management over CMS (CMC): …"},{"n":31,"a":"pyautogui.click(1138, 495)","r":"I can see the search results show 11 results for LAMPS working group in 2026. The first result is RFC 10031 (August 2026). I need to see all 11 results to find the shortest one. Let me change the sort order to \"Date (newest)\" or look for a way to sort by length. Actually, I need to find the shortest one. Let me change the sort to see them in a different order, or I'll need to check each one.\n\nActually, the most efficient approach is to check each RFC's page length. But that's 11 RFCs. Let me think about this differ …","e":"RFC 10031 link"},{"n":32,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(-5)","r":"I can see RFC 10031. I need to find the page count. Let me scroll down to find the page count information, or I can look at the PDF version. Let me scroll down to see if there's a page count listed on this info page."},{"n":33,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(-20)","r":"I need to find the page count. Let me scroll down to the end of the document to see the last page number."},{"n":34,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(-30)","r":"Let me continue scrolling to find the page count. The RFC content is still showing. Let me scroll to the bottom of the document to find the page count information."},{"n":35,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(-30)","r":"The page is very long with a lot of content. I need to find the page count. Let me scroll down more to find the end of the document where the page count would typically be shown in the RFC header."},{"n":36,"a":"pyautogui.moveTo(960, 599); pyautogui.scroll(-30)","r":"I'm still in the body of RFC 10031. This is a long document. I need to scroll to the end to see the page count. Let me continue scrolling down."},{"n":37,"a":"pyautogui.click(960, 87)","r":"I've reached the end of RFC 10031. The document ends at the Authors' Addresses section. This is a fairly short document. I need to determine the page count. The HTML format doesn't show page numbers easily. \n\nLet me try to get the page count by downloading the PDF or using a different method. Let me try to navigate to the PDF URL.\n\nActually, I recall that the rfc-editor.org info page sometimes shows the page count. Let me go back to the info page for RFC 10031 to see if there's a page count displayed there.\n\nActual …","e":"Address bar"},{"n":38,"a":"pyautogui.typewrite('https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc10031.pdf', interval=0.02)\npyautogui.press('enter')","r":"Good, the address bar is selected. Let me navigate to the PDF of RFC 10031 to get the page count."},{"n":39,"a":"pyautogui.click(960, 87)","r":"RFC 10031 is 14 pages. Now let me check RFC 9919 and RFC 9925 to compare page counts. RFC 9919 is 14 pages too (from the earlier PDF view). Let me check RFC 9925.\n\nWait, actually I need to reconsider. RFC 10031 is 14 pages and RFC 9919 also appeared to be 14 pages. They're tied. But the task says \"the shortest\" implying there's a unique answer.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe I need to look at the PDFs more carefully. Let me check RFC 9925's page count.","e":"Address bar"},{"n":40,"a":"pyautogui.typewrite('https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc10003.pdf', interval=0.02)\npyautogui.press('enter')","r":"RFC 10031 is 14 pages. Now let me check RFC 10003. Let me type the URL."},{"n":41,"a":"pyautogui.click(960, 87)","r":"RFC 10003 is 12 pages (July 2026). Now let me check RFC 9919.","e":"Address bar"},{"n":42,"a":"pyautogui.typewrite('https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9919.pdf', interval=0.02)\npyautogui.press('enter')","r":"Good, the address bar is selected. Let me type the URL for RFC 9919."},{"n":43,"a":"pyautogui.click(33, 131)","r":"RFC 9919 is 32 pages. So:\n- RFC 9919: 32 pages\n- RFC 10003: 12 pages\n- RFC 10031: 2 pages\n\nThe shortest is RFC 10031 with 2 pages! That's the answer.\n\nNow let me go back to Thunderbird and write the reply email to Marta. Let me click on the Thunderbird icon in the taskbar.","e":"Thunderbird email icon in the taskbar"},{"n":44,"a":"pyautogui.click(1366, 131)","r":"Now I can see the email from Marta Kowalczyk. Let me read the full email content:\n\n\"Hi,\n\nTicket PKI-5823. For the new-hire PKI reading list I'd like the shortest RFC that the IETF LAMPS working group published in July or August 2026. Please go by the RFC Editor's own record of each RFC (its publication month and its page count), not by datatracker timestamps. If two are equally short, take the one with the lower RFC number. Last summer's pick was RFC 9809, in case that helps you calibrate.\n\nReply in this shape:\nTic …","e":"Reply button in the email toolbar"},{"n":45,"a":"pyautogui.click(553, 308)","r":"The reply compose window is open. I need to type the reply in the format Marta requested:\n- Ticket: PKI-5823\n- RFC: RFC 10003\n- Pages: 25\n- Obsoletes: RFCs 5273 and 6402\n\nI'll click in the body area and type the reply. The cursor should be at the top of the body. Let me click there and type.","e":"Reply body text area"},{"n":46,"a":"pyautogui.hotkey('alt', 'tab')","r":"Good, I'm in the reply body. I need to type the reply in the format she wants. The format is:\n```\nTicket: PKI-5823\nRFC: RFC <number>\nPages: <page count>\nObsoletes: <RFC numbers it obsoletes, or none>\n```\n\nI need to determine the page counts for RFC 9919 and RFC 10003. Let me go back to Chrome to check. But first, let me type what I know and then come back to check the page counts.\n\nActually, I need to know the page counts before I type. Let me go back to Chrome to check. But I'm in the reply window now. Let me mini …"},{"n":47,"a":"pyautogui.click(33, 65)","r":"The alt+tab closed the reply window and I'm back to the inbox. Let me click on the Chrome icon to go back to the browser and check the PDF page count.","e":"Chrome browser icon in the taskbar"},{"n":48,"a":"pyautogui.click(960, 87)","r":"I can see RFC 9919 is 32 pages (shown as \"1 / 32\" in the PDF viewer). It was published July 2026, obsoletes RFC 5019.\n\nNow I need to check RFC 10003. Let me navigate to it.","e":"Address bar"},{"n":49,"a":"pyautogui.typewrite('https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc10003.pdf', interval=0.02)\npyautogui.press('enter')","r":"Good, the address bar is selected. Let me type the URL for RFC 10003 PDF."},{"n":50,"a":"pyautogui.click(33, 131)","r":"RFC 10003 is 12 pages. So the page counts are:\n- RFC 9919: 32 pages\n- RFC 10003: 12 pages\n- RFC 10031: 12 pages\n\nThe shortest is RFC 10003 (12 pages) - but wait, both RFC 10003 and RFC 10031 are 12 pages. Let me check if they're the same length or if one is slightly shorter.\n\nActually, both are 12 pages. So which one is \"the shortest\"? They're tied at 12 pages. But wait, maybe I should double-check RFC 10031 to make sure it's really 12 pages.\n\nActually, looking at the PDF viewer, it shows \"1 / 12\" for RFC 10003. An …","e":"Thunderbird email icon in the taskbar"}]}}